  "query_preview": "..\n\nHigh pressure drifts across the Appalachians toward the mid Atlantic \nbetween now and Thursday beneath anomalous ridging aloft. 500mb \nheights are at their maximum in the climatology space in both the \nNAEFS and ENS percentile calculations as geopotential heights peak \n~585 dam. The ridge/surface high pair maintains a pocket of dry air \nacross most of eastern CONUS, keeping SE Michigan dry through the \nwork week. Will have to watch for patchy fog potential again \nWednesday morning as the ridge axis moves overhead, establishing \nefficient radiative cooling across the area. \n\nSeasonable warmth is also expected through most of the period as \ncolumn thickness increases and warm advection persists. Guidance \ntoday has been running on the cool side, so boosted highs for \nWednesday toward the 60 degree mark. Upper 50s-low 60s highs \ncontinue into the weekend before a strong surge of cold advection \nearly next week. \n\nA sharp delineation in cloud cover over the Plains marks the eastern \nedge of return flow, which will be dislodged mid-week as a Pacific \nwave lifts around the high. Main height fall center and moisture \ntransport are directed well north of SE Michigan Thursday, although \nthe leading 35-40 knot low level jet results in a breezy day (25 to \n30 mph gusts). This is the start of the pattern breakdown, with the \nheight field flattening out by the end of the work week. The less \namplified pattern positions SE Michigan in the path of the next \nPacific wave to arrive this weekend. Dprog/dt has trended toward a \nweaker and slower wave, though this change appears to be tied to \nphase interactions between multiple embedded perturbations. These \ninteractions often carry lower predictability though so would not be \nsurprised to see more iterations to the low/frontal timing for the \nweekend. Highest confidence item in this part of the forecast is an \neventual cool down with strong cold advection behind the frontal \npassage, dropping highs into the 40s. Much more uncertainty exists \nregarding rain chances, especially timing.",
